Verdict: PASS. The fix works: on this runner, util-linux's sg no longer passes for ast-grep. I wrote the verdict to session_plan/eval_task_1.md.

What I ran: this runner has /usr/bin/sg, and sg --version prints sg from util-linux 2.41.3. There is no ast-grep installed. The bug was that yoyo took this sg for ast-grep.
• ./target/debug/yoyo ast foo printed "ast-grep (sg) is not installed." and exited 2. Before the fix it failed with "unrecognized option '--pattern'".
• ./target/debug/yoyo map --all ended with "6499 symbols across 253 files (using regex)", so it no longer claims to be using ast-grep.

What the diff does:
• The check: one check, run once per process, tries ast-grep first, then sg. It accepts a program only if --version exits 0 and the first line starts with the word ast-grep. Both places that used to start sg directly now go through it. A search for Command::new("sg") in src finds nothing left.
• Tests:
  • A table test rejects the exact util-linux line and accepts ast-grep 0.39.5.
  • Tests of the probe order cover four cases: only util-linux sg, a real ast-grep installed only as sg, both installed (where sg is never tried), and a failed exit status.
  • The old test that only asserted when sg was missing now always asserts something.
  • A new integration test uses a fake util-linux sg and checks for exit 2 and the not-installed message.
• Line counts: the symbols.rs edit swaps one line for one line, so its frozen 3804-line count is unchanged.
• Leftovers: every new function and constant is used in this diff, and no new enum variants were added.
• Docs: docs/src/usage/commands.md now says how ast-grep is found and that util-linux sg counts as not installed, in both the exit-code paragraph and the /map paragraph.

The fix doesn't touch the not-installed message, so it still says "ast-grep (sg)" even though yoyo now looks for ast-grep first.
